The Royal Australian Air Force (RAAF) operated several pilot training units during World War II, including No. 8 Elementary Flying Training School (No. 8 EFTS), No. 5 Elementary Flying Training School (No. 5 EFTS), and No. 3 Elementary Flying Training School (No. 3 EFTS). 

No. 8 EFTS was established in September 1940 at Narrandera, NSW, and ceased training in December 1944. 
No. 5 EFTS was established in June 1940 at Narromine, NSW, primarily operating Tiger Moths, and ceased training in June 1944. 
No. 3 EFTS was established in January 1940 at Essendon, Victoria, and was disbanded in May 1942, with its aircraft and staff transferred to No. 11 Elementary Flying School at Benalla.

These schools were part of the Empire Air Training Scheme, providing introductory flight instruction to new pilots. 

Post-WWII, the RAAF established No. 1 Basic Flying Training School (No. 1 BFTS) in December 1951 at RAAF Base Uranquinty, NSW. 
No. 1 BFTS was relocated to RAAF Base Point Cook, Victoria in May 1958, and was re-formed as No. 1 Flying Training School in January 1969.

Relevant locations include Essendon Airport, located in Melbourne, Victoria, approximately 13 km from Melbourne's City Centre, which was used by the RAAF and replaced by Tullamarine Airport as Melbourne's main airport in 1970.

Key dates include: 
- 1940, when No. 3 EFTS, No. 8 EFTS, and No. 5 EFTS were established; 
- 1942, when No. 3 EFTS was disbanded; 
- 1944, when No. 8 EFTS and No. 5 EFTS ceased training; 
- 1951, when No. 1 BFTS was established; 
- 1958, when No. 1 BFTS was relocated; 
- 1969, when No. 1 BFTS was re-formed as No. 1 Flying Training School; 
- 1970, when Essendon Airport was replaced by Tullamarine Airport.
